      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate markdown carefully.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Number: R137/2012-NL1-17.01\n- Scheme: This is an OIML certificate. The scheme field asks for A|B. R137 certificates... OIML-CS has scheme A and B. R137-1 (2012) — gas meters under OIML-CS. Actually the certificate number format \"R137/2012-NL1-17.01\" is typical of OIML MAA/certificates. NMi as Issuing Authority NL1. For R137, certificates can be under the OIML Certificate System (scheme A typically). Hard to determine definitively. The schema says scheme (A|B). Since it's not explicitly stated, I might omit it. Rule 8 says don't fabricate. I'll omit scheme or leave it out.\n- Project number: SO16200998\n- Page total: 5\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 23 June 2017 → 2017-06-23\n- OIML issuer id: NL1\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V. (OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\n- Address: Hugo de Grootplein 1, 3314 EG Dordrecht, the Netherlands\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman\n- Person title: Head Certification Board\n- Phone: +31 78 6332332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- OIML issuer id: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- ELGAS, s.r.o., Semtínská 211, Pardubice, Czech Republic (Applicant and manufacturer)\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Same as applicant: ELGAS, s.r.o.\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Gas meter\" (turbine gas meter)\n- Type designations: EMT-L, EMT-S, EMT-Lx, EMT-Dc\n- Module designation: Not applicable? The cert says electromagnetic class not applicable (meter has no electronics). Module designation — probably omit or null.\n- Description: turbine gas meter\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- id: R137 (canonical). Actually \"R 137-1 (2012)\" — the recommendation is R137-1. Canonical form... The example says \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". So \"R137-1\" perhaps. Edition: 2012? The edition field is int. Hmm, R137-1 (2012) — edition 2012? Typically edition would be 1 for R137-1... Actually R137-1 is the first edition published 2012. The certificate says \"R 137-1 (2012)\". I'll use edition: 2012? The schema says edition (int). For R60 certs, edition is like 2000 (OIML R 60-1 2000). Hmm, edition is usually the year of the edition in these cases or the edition number. I think using 2012 is reasonable. Actually let me think — \"edition (int)\" — for OIML R 60 (2000), edition would be... ambiguous. I'll put 2012.\n- amendment: null — omit.\n- scheme: omit (unknown).\n- accuracy_classes: [\"1\"]\n\n**Test reports:**\n- NMi-14200104-01, 15 September 2014, 14 pages, type_evaluation_report (the cert says \"associated (OIML Type Evaluation) Reports\")\n- NMi-14200702-01, 18 December 2014, 13 pages\n- NMi-14200712-01, 23 December 2014, 35 pages\n- VSL-3255499-01, 28 January 2015, 6 pages\n\nRole: they're all labeled \"(OIML Type Evaluation) Reports\" → role: type_evaluation_report.\n\n**Documentation file:** Documentation folder no. T10775-0 → test_reports entry with role documentation_file? The schema says test_reports[]: id, date, pages, role; roles include documentation_file. T10775-0 has no date/pages. I could include it: {\"id\": \"T10775-0\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}. Yes.\n\n**Revision history:** none mentioned. Omit.\n\n**Footnotes:** none. Omit.\n\n**Characteristics:**\n\nPage 2 Table 1 (general characteristics for all types):\n- Destined for the measurement of: Gas volume\n- Mechanical class: M1\n- Electromagnetic class: Not applicable (the meter has no electronics)\n- Ambient temperature range: -40 °C / +70 °C → {min: -40, max: 70}, unit °C\n- Gas temperature range: -40 °C / +70 °C\n- Orientation: Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down (all orientations) → string value \"all orientations\" or \"Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down\"\n- Flow direction: Uni-directional (indicated with arrow)\n\nThese are type_level.\n\nPage 3 and 4: Table 1 (continued, main characteristics matrix) and Table 2. These are big matrices with per-configuration rows: each row is Type × DN body × DN cartridge with three G-value/Qmax/Qt/Qmin variants (MR 1:20, MR 1:30, MR 1:50 measurement ratios / pressure ranges).\n\nThis is complex. The tables list for each type/DN combination three rows of values (stacked via <br>), corresponding to three measurement ratio (MR) columns:\n- MR 1:20 (0..101 bar(g)) — only on page 3 table\n- MR 1:30 (4..101 bar(g))\n- MR 1:50 (8..101 bar(g)) — page 3 only; page 4 has 0..101 for standard and 4..101 columns... wait let me re-read.\n\nPage 3 table columns:\n- Type, DN body (mm), DN cartr. (mm), G-value, Qmax (m³/h), Qt (m³/h), Qmin under MR 1:20 0..101 bar(g) (m³/h), Qmin under MR 1:30 4..101 bar(g) (m³/h), Qmin under MR 1:50 8..101 bar(g) (m³/h), Number of drums before and after comma, Control element (m³/h), Blade angle (degree), Blade qty, Bearing dimensions standard bearings 0..101 bar(g) front/rear, Optional bearings 4..101 bar(g) front/rear.\n\nPage 4 table (Table 2):\n- Same but Qmin columns: 0..101 bar(g) with MR 1:30 and MR 1:50 (from sub-header: \"0..101 bar(g)\" spans two columns and \"4..101 bar(g)\" one; then MR 1:30, MR 1:50 row). So Table 2 has Qmin for MR 1:30 and MR 1:50 only (larger meters).\n\nEach cell in G-value, Qmax, Qt, Qmin columns contains three stacked values, corresponding to the three G-value rows... Actually the first stacked value corresponds to G-value first entry. E.g., row 1: G-value 100/160/250, Qmax 160/250/400, Qt 32/50/80, Qmin(MR1:20) 8/12.5/20, Qmin(MR1:30) 5/8/12.5, Qmin(MR1:50) 3/5/8.\n\nSo each row actually encodes 3 meter variants: (G100, Qmax160, Qt32, Qmin8/5/3), (G160, Qmax250, Qt50, Qmin12.5/8/5), (G250, Qmax400, Qt80, Qmin20/12.5/8).\n\nBlade angle 55/45/35 and blade qty 12/14/16 also stacked correspondingly. So variant G100 has blade angle 55, qty 12; G160 → 45°, 14; G250 → 35°, 16.\n\nHow to model this in the schema? Options:\n\n1. model_family.models with model_id per variant like \"EMT-L_DN80_G100\" etc. That would be a lot of models (each row × 3 = many).\n\n2. matrix_tables: preserve the tables as-is with rows. That's the \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" option.\n\n3. model_level characteristics with attribute × model.\n\nGiven the complexity, I think the best approach is:\n- matrix_tables[] with the full page 3 table (\"Main characteristics — Table 1 (page 3)\") and page 4 table (\"Table 2\"), preserving the stacked values as strings (e.g. \"160/250/400\") since flattening them into separate rows would require interpretation. Hmm, but rule says preserve multi-dim tables. The rows can be objects. The stacked cells contain multiple values separated by line breaks. I could keep them as strings like \"160<br>250<br>400\" → \"160/250/400\". Numbers as strings are acceptable in matrix_tables rows? The schema example shows numbers. But stacked values are lists. I'll keep them as strings with \"/\" separator to preserve fidelity, or expand each row into 3 sub-rows.\n\nActually, let me think about being maximally useful: expanding each table row into 3 variant rows would be the most informative. E.g.:\n\nRow: Type EMT-Lx/EMT-Dc, DN body 50, DN cartr 80:\n- G100: Qmax 160, Qt 32, Qmin 8 (MR1:20), 5 (MR1:30), 3 (MR1:50), drums 7/1, control 0.02, blade angle 55, blade qty 12, bearings...\n- G160: Qmax 250, Qt 50, Qmin 12.5, 8, 5, blade 45, qty 14\n- G250: Qmax 400, Qt 80, Qmin 20, 12.5, 8, blade 35, qty 16\n\nBut bearing dimensions are single values per row (not stacked), so same for all three variants.\n\nThis is doable but the output would be huge. The instruction says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. I think preserving with stacked strings is acceptable and safer, plus model_level characteristics for the main flow attributes.\n\nHmm. Let me decide on approach:\n\nOption A: model_family with models keyed by type+DN+G. That's ~13 rows × 3 = 39 variants for page 3 + 14 rows × 3 = 42 for page 4 ≈ 81 models. Way too much.\n\nOption B: matrix_tables preserving rows as in the source (with stacked string values), plus model_level entries not attempted for these (since attributes vary by configuration more than by model). Maybe include a few model_level entries keyed by type? E.g., attribute \"qmax\" with values per model \"EMT-L\"? But qmax varies within a type across DN sizes. So model_level at type granularity is wrong.\n\nI think the cleanest: use matrix_tables for both tables (page 3 Table 1 and page 4 Table 2), with rows expanded into per-G variant rows where feasible, OR keep stacked strings.\n\nGiven rule 9's emphasis on parseability and the schema note \"matrix_tables[]: preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\", I'll preserve tables with stacked values as strings, splitting stacked values with \"/\". Actually, let me reconsider — expanding would give numeric values which are more useful downstream. But risk of misalignment is low since the stacking is consistent (3 values per cell aligned with 3 G-values). Except blade angle in Table 2: \"45<br>45 / 30<br>30\" — that's 3 values: 45, 45/30, 30. And blade qty 24,24,24. Drums 8/0, control 0.2.\n\nHmm, \"45 / 30\" is a single variant's blade angle being \"45 / 30\"? Looking: `45<br>45 / 30<br>30` → values: \"45\", \"45 / 30\", \"30\". So middle variant has blade angle 45/30 (two possible angles?). Odd but preserve as string.\n\nI'll go with matrix_tables preserving stacked strings, using \"/\" separator. Also add description noting each cell contains three values corresponding to the three G-values in order.\n\nAlternatively, expand rows. Let me weigh: The consumer of this JSON wants structured data. The schema's model_level wants attribute × model matrix. Honestly for this certificate, the matrix_tables route is explicitly provided for this situation. I'll do both: matrix_tables (faithful), plus model_level for the type-level characteristics that vary cleanly? I think adding a model_level at the granularity of \"type+DN\" would be messy.\n\nLet me also capture general characteristics (Table on page 2) in type_level:\n\n- measurement_quantity: \"Gas volume\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- electromagnetic_class: \"Not applicable\" (meter has no electronics) — value string \"Not applicable\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min: -40, max: 70}, °C\n- gas_temperature_range: {min: -40, max: 70}, °C\n- orientation: \"all orientations\" (horizontal / vertical up / vertical down)\n- flow_direction: \"Uni-directional\"\n- accuracy_class: 1 (type_level) — though it's in recommendation too.\n- max_pressure: 0..101 bar(g)? That's per table column headers, describing pressure ranges for Qmin measurement ratios. Hmm, the \"0..101 bar(g)\" appears in column sub-headers. It's a condition, not a simple characteristic. Could add config_level? Probably too noisy. I'll mention pressure ranges in matrix table descriptions.\n\nModel family: The cert covers 4 types: EMT-L, EMT-S, EMT-Lx, EMT-Dc. Is that a \"model family\"? The schema says include model_family only if cert covers >1 model variant. It does. family_name: null. models: [{\"model_id\": \"EMT-L\"}, {\"model_id\": \"EMT-S\"}, {\"model_id\": \"EMT-Lx\"}, {\"model_id\": \"EMT-Dc\"}].\n\nModel_level characteristics: Are there attributes that vary cleanly per type? Installation conditions differ per type (page 5): straightener options and inlet pipe lengths:\n- EMT-L: two different flow straighteners; large → 1×DN straight inlet; small → 5×DN.\n- EMT-S: only large straightener → min 1×DN.\n- EMT-Lx: no straight inlet tubing requirements.\n- EMT-Dc: same as EMT-Lx.\n\nThis fits config_level or model_level. I could do config_level:\n- attribute: minimum_straight_inlet_pipe_length, axis: flow_straightener, values: [{condition: \"EMT-L with large flow straightener\", value: 1 (unit \"DN\")}, {condition: \"EMT-L with small flow straightener\", value: 5, unit \"DN\"}, {condition: \"EMT-S (large flow straightener only)\", value: 1, unit \"DN\"}, {condition: \"EMT-Lx\", value: null}...]\n\nHmm, value for EMT-Lx: no requirement. Could