8, 20x47x20, 10x26x8\nr38: G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200, \"45 / 30\", 24\nr39: G10000: 16000, 3200, 533, 320, 30, 24\nr40: EMT-S, EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc, 600, 600, 6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200, 45, 24, 20x47x20, 10x26x8, 20x47x20, 10x26x8\nr41: G10000: 16000, 3200, 533, 320, \"45 / 30\", 24\nr42: G16000: 25000, 5000, 800, 400, 30, 24\n\nNote about the \"80\" Qt: rows 16, 22, 28 (Table 2 rows with G2500 base at DN300/400, 400/400, 500/400) have qt \"80\" per source (likely OCR of 800). I'll keep 80? Hmm... I keep debating. Let me check the source again: `<td>2500<br>4000<br>6500</td><td>4000<br>6500<br>10000</td><td>80<br>1300<br>2000</td>` — yes Qt = 80/1300/2000. The correct value per R137 table logic is 800. But maybe the actual certificate has \"800\" and OCR dropped a zero. Preserving \"80\" records wrong data; \"correcting\" fabricates. Compromise: keep as printed but add table-level description note. Actually... you know, I could keep as printed and not note. Or keep printed and note. I'll note in description: \"Qt values for G2500 at DN300/400, DN400/400 and DN500/400 appear as '80' in the source (consistent with 800 = Qmax/5).\" That's transparent. Good.\n\nHmm wait, also check Table 1 row 6: G400/650/1000 → Qmax 650/1000/1600, Qt 130/200/320 ✓ consistent.\n\nNow, given the enormous tables, maybe I should reconsider whether to also keep model_level. I'll skip model_level entirely — matrix_tables carry the data. Actually wait — should I worry the grader specifically wants model_level? The rubric example was R60-style. For this doc, matrix_tables is the documented escape. I'll add a brief note? No notes outside schema. Fine.\n\nComponents: Should I add? The essential parts named: drums, control element, blades, bearings. I'll add components entries:\n\n[\n {\"role\": \"measuring drum set\", \"characteristics\":?} — no clean data without model context.\n\nI'll skip components — data lives in tables. Actually rule 6: \"For complex instruments... record each as a components[] entry\". The gas meter here is a standalone mechanical meter; essential parts are internal (drums, rotor blades, bearings) without independent type designations. I'll omit components to avoid noise. Hmm, but bearings have \"standard\" vs \"optional\" alternatives... captured in table columns. OK omit.\n\nNow date formats: date_issued \"2017-06-23\". Test report dates ISO.\n\ncertificate.scheme: The number \"R137/2012-NL1-17.01\" — under OIML-CS, R137 certificates are issued under the OIML Certificate System for measuring instruments... Scheme A vs B: MAA (Mutual Acceptance Arrangement) — R137 is in MAA? OIML-CS scheme B covers recommendations listed in MAA (e.g., R137 gas meters might be under MAA since 2018?). Certificate from 2017 — before OIML-CS launch (2018?). Not stated in doc → omit. Good.\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\" — from \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\". Yes.\n\nLet me also double check \"date_issued\": \"23 June 2017\" → 2017-06-23. ✓\n\npage_total: 5.\n\nNow assemble final JSON. Keys order roughly per schema.\n\nFor type_level values, structured objects:\n\n\"measurement_quantity\": {\"value\": \"Gas volume\"}\n\"mechanical_class\": {\"value\": \"M1\"}\n\"electromagnetic_class\": {\"value\": \"Not applicable\"}\n\"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 70}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": \"u:degree_Celsius\"}\n\"gas_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 70}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": \"u:degree_Celsius\"}\n\"orientation\": {\"value\": \"Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down (all orientations)\"}\n\"flow_direction\": {\"value\": \"Uni-directional (indicated with arrow)\"}\n\nconfig_level:\n\n[\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"applicable_pressure_range\",\n  \"axis\": \"measurement_ratio\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"MR 1:20 (Table 1, page 3)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0, \"max\": 101}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"MR 1:30 (Table 1, page 3)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 4, \"max\": 101}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"MR 1:50 (Table 1, page 3)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 8, \"max\": 101}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"MR 1:30 (Table 2, page 4)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0, \"max\": 101}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"MR 1:50 (Table 2, page 4)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 4, \"max\": 101}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"applicable_pressure_range\",\n  \"axis\": \"bearing_type\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"standard bearings\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0, \"max\": 101}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"optional bearings\", \"value\": {\"min\": 4, \"max\": 101}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_straight_inlet_pipe_length\",\n  \"axis\": \"flow_straightener\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"EMT-L with large flow straightener\", \"value\": 1, \"unit_symbol\": \"DN\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"EMT-L with small flow straightener\", \"value\": 5, \"unit_symbol\": \"DN\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"EMT-S (large flow straightener only)\", \"value\": 1, \"unit_symbol\": \"DN\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"EMT-Lx\", \"value\": \"no straight inlet tubing requirement\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"EMT-Dc\", \"value\": \"no straight inlet tubing requirement (same as EMT-Lx)\"}\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"attribute\": \"flow_straightener_options\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter_type\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"EMT-L\", \"value\": \"large or small flow straightener\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"EMT-S\", \"value\": \"large flow straightener only\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"EMT-Lx\", \"value\": \"inlet flow straightener identical to EMT-S (identical cartridges)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"EMT-Dc\", \"value\": \"two EMT-Lx cartridges in one housing; same installation conditions and flow straighteners as EMT-Lx\"}\n  ]\n }\n]\n\nWait — for EMT-Dc \"The same installation conditions and flow straighteners are applicable for the EMT-Dc as given for the EMT-Lx.\" So Dc same as Lx (no straight tubing requirement). ✓\n\nmatrix_tables[0]:\n{\n \"name\": \"Table 1: Main characteristics of EMT-L, EMT-S, EMT-Lx and EMT-Dc (DN 50–250)\",\n \"columns\": [...17...],\n \"column_units\": [null,\"mm\",\"mm\",null,\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",null,\"m³/h\",\"degree\",null,\"mm\",\"mm\",\"mm\",\"mm\"],\n \"rows\": [...36 rows...],\n \"description\": \"Each source row lists three G-value variants (values stacked in cells correspond in order to the G-value entries). Qmin depends on measurement ratio MR (1:20, 1:30, 1:50) and pressure range. Number of drums '7 / 1 or 8 / 0' with control element '0.02 or 0.2' indicates two alternative registrations/drums options. Bearing dimensions are inside diameter x outside diameter x width.\",\n \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nmatrix_tables[1]:\n{\n \"name\": \"Table 2: Main characteristics of EMT-S, EMT-Lx and EMT-Dc (DN 200–600, G ≥ 1000)\",\n \"columns\": [16 columns],\n \"column_units\": [...],\n \"rows\": [...42...],\n \"description\": \"Meters of sizes DN 200–600; no MR 1:20 variant. Qmin MR 1:30 applies for 0–101 bar(g) and MR 1:50 for 4–101 bar(g). Standard bearings 0–101 bar(g); optional bearings 4–101 bar(g). Blade angle '45 / 30' for G1600 variants as printed. Qt printed as '80' for G2500 rows of DN300/400, DN400/400 and DN500/400 (consistent with 800 m³/h = Qmax/5).\",\n \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nHmm — is Table 2 limited to EMT-S/Lx/Dc? Types: \"EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc\", \"EMT-S, EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc\" — no EMT-L. Yes, DN≥200 rows never include EMT-L. Name accordingly.\n\nDrums value: \"7 / 1\" means 7 drums before decimal, 1 after. Keep string \"7 / 1\". And \"7 / 1 or 8 / 0\". Page 4: \"8 / 0\".\n\nControl element: 0.02 with unit m³/h (per units row). Numeric 0.02, or \"0.02 or 0.2\" string for dual rows.\n\nBlade angle numeric for single, string \"45 / 30\" for dual.\n\nNow write full JSON. Be careful with numbers: 12.5, 32.5, 0.02, 0.2 decimals.\n\nType strings: \"EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc\", \"EMT-L, EMT-S, EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc\", \"EMT-S, EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc\".\n\nLet me finalize row arrays.\n\nTable 1 rows (36). I'll write them compactly but fully:\n\n1 {\"type\":\"EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc\",\"dn_body\":50,\"dn_cartridge\":80,\"g_value\":100,\"qmax\":160,\"qt\":32,\"qmin_mr_1_20\":8,\"qmin_mr_1_30\":5,\"qmin_mr_1_50\":3,\"drums_before_after_decimal\":\"7 / 1\",\"control_element\":0.02,\"blade_angle\":55,\"blade_quantity\":12,\"bearing_standard_front\":\"3x6x3\",\"bearing_standard_rear\":\"2x5x2\",\"bearing_optional_front\":\"3x8x4\",\"bearing_optional_rear\":\"3x6x3\"}\n2 G160: qmax250 qt50 qmin 12.5/8/5 blade45 qty14 (others same)\n3 G250: qmax400 qt80 qmin 20/12.5/8 blade35 qty16\n4 {\"type\":\"EMT-L, EMT-S, EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc\",\"dn_body\":80,\"dn_cartridge\":80,\"g_value\":100,\"qmax\":160,\"qt\":32,\"qmin\":8,5,3,\"7 / 1\",0.02,55,12,\"3x6x3\",\"2x5x2\",\"3x8x4\",\"3x6x3\"}\n5 G160 → 250,50,12.5,8,5,45,14\n6 G250 → 400,80,20,12.5,8,35,16\n7 {\"type\":\"EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc\",\"dn_body\":80,\"dn_cartridge\":100,\"g_value\":160,\"qmax\":250,\"qt\":50,\"qmin\":12.5,8,5,\"7 / 1\",0.02,55,12,\"3x8x4\",\"3x6x3\",\"5x10x3\",\"3x6x3\"}\n8 G250 → 400,80,20,12.5,8,45,14\n9 G400 → 650,130,32.5,20,12.5,35,16\n10 {\"type\":\"EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc\",\"dn_body\":100,\"dn_cartridge\":80,\"g_value\":100,...,\"3x6x3\",\"2x5x2\",\"3x8x4\",\"3x6x3\"} qmax160 qt32 qmin8/5/3 blade55 qty12\n11 G160 → 250,50,12.5,8,5,45,14\n12 G250 → 400,80,20,12.5,8,35,16\n13 {\"type\":\"EMT-L, EMT-S, EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc\",\"dn_body\":100,\"dn_cartridge\":100,\"g_value\":160,\"qmax\":250,\"qt\":50,qmin12.5/8/5,\"7 / 1\",0.02,55,12,\"3x8x4\",\"3x6x3\",\"5x10x3\",\"3x6x3\"}\n14 G250 → 400,80,20,12.5,8,45,14\n15 G400 → 650,130,32.5,20,12.5,35,16\n16 {\"type\":\"EMT-Lx or EMT-Dc\",\"dn_body\":100,\"dn_cartridge\":150,\"g_value\":400,\"qmax\":650,\"qt\":130,qmin32.5/20/12.5,\"7 / 1 or 8 / 0\",\"0.02 or 0.2\",55,12,\"5x11x5\",\"5x11x5\",\"5x16x5\",\"5x11x5\"}\n